## Checklist (kind: general)

- Before starting, state what counts as done.
- At the end, prove it is done.
- In Evidence, name each new test and how it failed before your change and passes after.
- Do not make up a fact you do not have (a URL, a price, a name, a version, an API field, a config value). Write a visible placeholder instead, `<<PLACEHOLDER: what is missing>>`, and list each one in Not done / uncertain.
- When the job covers requirements, end each commit message with a trailer naming the ones it touches: `Requirements: R01, R03i`.
- A part the brief does not allow (outside its scope, needs access you lack, breaks a rule) is not done. Write one line for it in Not done / uncertain: `BLOCKED: <requirement or item> · <why>`. A covered requirement you blocked is `missing` or `partial` in the `### Requirements` block.
- Heavy steps (a full test suite, a type check, a build, `scripts/check.sh`) run through the box-wide gate lock, so they never overlap with the heavy steps of other jobs and of the orchestrator: `agent-jobs gate run --head HEAD --repo . -- <command>` (`--repo .` is the current directory; the command and its arguments follow `--` as you would write them). It waits for the lock, and that wait does not count against your timeout; its last line is `exit=N`, the command's exit code. Never run a gate inside another gate (also not in an acceptance script) or two gates at once: gates do not nest, so an inner gate waits for the lock its parent holds and times out without running its command, and a second gate of the same job waits for the first. If it exits 125 with a `gate:` note that the lock cannot be opened (not one that it is held), run the command directly once and say so in Not done / uncertain. A single test or a quick check of one package runs directly.
- UI changes: when your diff touches UI (css/scss, html, jsx/tsx, vue/svelte, templates, static-site renderers, design tokens), before you report done load every changed page in a real headless browser. The default tool is `node <job dir>/tools/ui-check.mjs <base-url> <path>... --out <job dir>/ui-check` (Playwright from your environment; serve the site locally first); the repo's own e2e tool is fine if it covers the same. Device matrix at least 360x740, 390x844, 768x1024, 1366x768, 1920x1080; light and dark scheme when the site has both. Check the full page length (scroll to the end so lazy content loads), not just the first screen: no horizontal page scroll (`documentElement.scrollWidth <= clientWidth`), no visible element sticking out of the viewport outside a scroll container, no broken images, CLS < 0.1, no word split inside a word (e.g. a table cell narrower than its longest word), no 4xx/5xx responses. Save the screenshots in the job dir and list every device x scheme x page with pass/fail in Evidence. If you cannot start a browser (no sockets, no child processes, e.g. the Codex sandbox, or missing system libraries; ui-check.mjs exits 2), «Not done / uncertain» MUST start with `BROWSER CHECK NOT RUN: <reason>`; never claim the UI is verified.

## Report contract

Your FINAL message is the report. It is saved verbatim as `report.md`; a job that ends without it is marked failed. It is a claim, not a result: the orchestrator will verify it.

Use exactly these four sections, in this order, then a Forks section and a Progress section:

```
## Summary
<2-5 lines: what was asked, what you did, the outcome>

## Findings / Changes
<findings (review/research) or changes made (fix/general), with file:line and commit hashes>

## Evidence
<commands run and their relevant output, tests, sources opened>

## Not done / uncertain
<what you did not do, could not verify, or are unsure about; write "nothing" only if truly nothing>

## Forks
<up to 5 entries, one per line: "choice / rejected alternative / reason"; write `none` if the task left no room for a choice>

## Progress
<one line per step you took, oldest first: "- HH:MM UTC <what you did>"; take the time from `date -u +%H:%M`, or write "--:--" when you do not know it>
```

"Not done / uncertain" is mandatory and is read first. Forks names the design choices you made where the task left room (a coin flip taken silently is what a reviewer cannot see in the diff): the choice, the alternative you rejected, and why; at most 5, the ones that matter most. Progress is read after them: keep each step to one line.
